 ## Overview
-A Transfer Function (TF) maps raw scalar voxel values from a 3D volume dataset to optical properties—specifically color (RGB) and opacity ($\alpha$)—enabling the classification and visualization of specific structures [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S6, unofficial][S8, unofficial][S9, unofficial][S13, unofficial]. It solves the problem of "visual clutter" by allowing users to hide irrelevant data (e.g., air, background) and emphasize regions of interest (e.g., bones, vessels) through selective transparency [S1, unofficial][S6, unofficial][S8, unofficial]. Unlike multi-dimensional TFs that incorporate gradient magnitude or other derivatives, the 1D TF relies solely on the scalar density value, making it the simplest classification tool [S2, unofficial][S8, unofficial][S12, unofficial].
+A Transfer Function (TF) maps raw scalar voxel values from a 3D volume dataset to optical properties—specifically color (RGB) and opacity ($\alpha$)—enabling the classification and visualization of specific structures [S1, S3, S6, S8, S9, unofficial][S13, unofficial]. It solves the problem of "visual clutter" by allowing users to hide irrelevant data (e.g., air, background) and emphasize regions of interest (e.g., bones, vessels) through selective transparency [S1, S6, S8, unofficial]. Unlike multi-dimensional TFs that incorporate gradient magnitude or other derivatives, the 1D TF relies solely on the scalar density value, making it the simplest classification tool [S2, S8, S12, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A scalar voxel value $s(x)$ (e.g., CT Hounsfield units, density) [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial][S7, unofficial][S8, unofficial].
-*   **Output:** A 4-tuple $(R, G, B, \alpha)$ representing the color and opacity for that scalar value [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S6, unofficial][S8, unofficial][S9, unofficial].
