 ### [Transfer Function Mapping via 1D Textures](8608-transfer-function-mapping-via-1d-textures.md)
-Maps voxel scalar density values to RGBA attributes using pre-sampled 1D textures. It distinguishes itself from multi-dimensional TFs by relying solely on density, whereas siblings like [Gradient-based Transfer Function](1273-gradient-based-transfer-function.md) incorporate gradient magnitude. Choose this method when direct GPU texture sampling is preferred over UI manipulation.
+This method maps voxel scalar density values to RGBA attributes using pre-sampled 1D textures. It distinguishes itself from multi-dimensional TFs by relying solely on density, whereas siblings like [Gradient-based Transfer Function](1273-gradient-based-transfer-function.md) incorporate gradient magnitude. It is distinguished from the [1D Transfer Function Editor](8746-1d-transfer-function-editor.md) by its direct texture sampling implementation rather than UI-based control point manipulation.
 
 ### [1D Transfer Function Editor](8746-1d-transfer-function-editor.md)
-A user interface component that maps scalar voxel values to optical properties using a 1D curve editor with control points. It solves the problem of interactive classification by allowing users to visually define visibility. Choose this method when interactive, real-time control point manipulation is required.
+A user interface component that maps scalar voxel values to optical properties using a 1D curve editor with control points. It solves the problem of interactive classification by allowing users to visually define visibility. It is distinguished from [Transfer Function Mapping via 1D Textures](8608-transfer-function-mapping-via-1d-textures.md) by its explicit UI-based control point manipulation, and from [Piecewise Constant Transfer Function](12134-piecewise-constant-transfer-function.md) by its support for continuous interpolation between knots.
 
 ### [Piecewise Constant Transfer Function](12134-piecewise-constant-transfer-function.md)
-Maps voxel intensity values to RGBA using disjoint, non-overlapping intervals, creating a "step" response [G]. It distinguishes itself from the parent [Transfer Function](1272-transfer-function.md) by its lack of interpolation (binary classification) [G]. Choose this method when strict threshold-based material inclusion/exclusion is needed without blending [G].
+Maps voxel intensity values to RGBA using disjoint, non-overlapping intervals, creating a "step" response [12134-piecewise-constant-transfer-function.md]. It is distinguished from the parent [Transfer Function](1272-transfer-function.md) by its lack of interpolation (binary classification) [12134-piecewise-constant-transfer-function.md]. It differs from [Transfer Function Mapping via 1D Textures](8608-transfer-function-mapping-via-1d-textures.md) by not requiring texture sampling or linear interpolation [12134-piecewise-constant-transfer-function.md].
 
 ### 3D Pre-integrated Transfer Function
-A 3D texture-based TF used during optical property accumulation to handle varying sampling frequencies and constant brick values [S19, unofficial]. It stores the function in a 3D texture accessed via floating-point coordinates [S19, unofficial]. Choose this method when adaptive sampling is used and constant brightness across varying step sizes is required [S19, unofficial].
+A 3D texture-based TF used during optical property accumulation to handle varying sampling frequencies and constant brick values [S19, unofficial]. It stores the function in a 3D texture accessed via floating-point coordinates [S19, unofficial]. It allows maintaining constant brightness and provides better rendering quality with adaptive sampling compared to standard TFs [S19, unofficial].
 
 ### Local Singular Transfer Function
-A local rendering approach using a lookup table to assign texture values (color/opacity) to interpolated points (IDs) prior to global composition [S20, unofficial]. It addresses the insufficiency of global multi-dimensional TFs for complex datasets [S20, unofficial]. Choose this method when object IDs are available and global classification fails to separate complex overlapping structures [S20, unofficial].
